h1: Who should see what in a solar company.
lead: Giving everyone access to everything is easy and risky. Giving everyone too little slows the work. This guide proposes a sensible starting point for each seat, which you can adjust to your business.
answer: Give each person the records and actions their job needs, keep cost and margin with the people who set prices, separate partners from internal data, and keep a record of sensitive actions.
published: 2026-10-11
sections:
  - h: Start from the job, not the title
    p:
      - Job titles vary between companies. List what each person actually does every day, and give access to those actions and the records they touch.
  - h: Sales
    p:
      - Sales staff need their own enquiries, the quotation tools and the customer's contact history. They rarely need purchase prices, vendor rates or margin.
  - h: Survey and field teams
    p:
      - Surveyors need the jobs assigned to them, the survey form and the ability to upload photos. Installers need the checklist stages they own and the materials issued to their jobs.
  - h: Project and operations managers
    p:
      - They need every live job in their area, the delivery stages, documents and material status. They benefit from seeing pending payments on their jobs, so the customer conversation is consistent.
  - h: Store
    p:
      - The storekeeper needs the catalogue, stock movements and project issue requests. They do not need customer pricing.
  - h: Accounts
    p:
      - Accounts needs claims, receipts, pending balances and exports. Keep the person who raises a claim separate from the person who approves it.
  - h: Channel partners
    p:
      - Partners should see only the leads they submitted, their status and their own commission. Keep them in a separate login so internal notes never reach them.
  - h: Owners and senior managers
    p:
      - Owners usually need the widest view, including reports across sales, delivery and money, and the ability to review sensitive actions.
  - h: Protect cost and margin
    p:
      - Treat internal costing as its own permission. A salesperson who can see margin may concede it, and a customer who overhears the number may ask for it.
  - h: Review access regularly
    p:
      - When people change role or leave, update their access the same day. Once a quarter, review who can export data, approve claims and see costing.
takeaways:
  - Access follows the daily job.
  - Costing, approval and export are separate, deliberate permissions.
  - Partners are separated from internal data.
